      <aside class="calc-sidebar">
        <div class="card sidebar-card">
          <h2>Absorption vs boiling method</h2>
          <p>The absorption method uses a measured amount of water that the rice absorbs completely during cooking — this is what this calculator is designed for. The boiling method uses a large pot of water that is drained afterwards, similar to cooking pasta, and does not require precise measurement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card sidebar-card">
          <h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>
          <details>
            <summary>Should I rinse rice before cooking?</summary>
            <p>Rinsing rice removes excess surface starch, which can make the rice less sticky and more separate. If you rinse, you may need slightly less water as the rice absorbs some during rinsing. Basmati and jasmine rice are commonly rinsed before cooking.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Does soaking rice affect how much water I need?</summary>
            <p>Yes. Soaking rice before cooking allows it to absorb water in advance, so you may need less water during cooking. Basmati rice is often soaked for 20-30 minutes before cooking, which can reduce the cooking water needed.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Why does my rice come out too wet or too dry?</summary>
            <p>The ratio is a starting point — altitude, pot size, heat level and whether the lid fits tightly all affect the result. If your rice is consistently too wet, reduce water by 10-15%. If it is too dry or burns at the bottom, add a little more water next time.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How much rice should I cook per person?</summary>
            <p>As a side dish, around 60-75g of uncooked rice per person is typical. As a main dish, 100-125g per person. Rice roughly doubles in weight when cooked, so 100g of dry rice yields approximately 200g of cooked rice.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
